    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Yeah, I know I'll need a pro for the final sign-off, I'm just trying to get a ballpark figure for my material costs right now. If I'm thinking roughly a 3x12 or a 4x12, do you think that's going to be enough to handle the deflection, or am I definitely going to be looking at something much beefier to keep that bounce down? Also, any specific things I should look for in the wood itself when I'm selecting the beams to avoid any structural issues later?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a 14-foot span, a 3x12 or 4x12 might be on the smaller side to strictly control deflection with a 30 psf live load. You might find that a larger section, perhaps something closer to a doubled or tripled beam system, is required to meet typical live load deflection limits. When selecting lumber, always insist on milled, fully dried (kiln-dried) softwood, and verify the stamped grade and species certification. Avoid using green or untreated wood, as moisture content variations can severely affect structural performance and induce warping.",
      "source": "target",
